#N canvas 40 40 1300 820 realtime-out 0;
#X text 20 20 The clock and the transport go to port 1 \, the Launchpad. Port 3 was the SP-404's \, whose BPM SYNC learned tempo from the pulse \, and gets nothing since the 404 left the rig (2026-11-04). Port 4 takes the panic STOP alone \, to the right., f 58;
#X obj 20 200 r \$0-rt;
#X obj 20 320 midiout;
#X obj 700 150 loadbang;
#X obj 700 195 t b b;
#X msg 700 250 1;
#X text 20 420 The port goes into the cold inlet at load: Pd 0.49 does not warn about an extra creation argument \, so one could not be confirmed (item 63)., f 58;
#X text 20 570 A port with nothing on it is dropped in silence \, so on the Mac this costs nothing., f 58;
#X text 20 690 One port \, so no trigger: C-3 is for a fan-out. The port-4 box has its own receive and is not fed from this one., f 58;
#X obj 580 200 r \$0-rt4;
#X obj 580 320 midiout;
#X msg 1020 250 4;
#X text 580 420 ⛔ PORT 4 GETS THE PANIC STOP AND NOT THE CLOCK \, and the separate receive is what makes that true. \$0-rt4 is written in exactly one place -- the panic branch on the parent canvas -- so no clock byte can reach it however this file is edited later. Item 279 measured ports 2 and 4 getting NOTHING at all \, which is a bug for the Volca: it is a synthesizer with a sequencer \, and one still running through a panic is the case panic exists for. ⚠️ WIDENING THE CLOCK TO MORE PORTS IS A SEPARATE QUESTION and is parked in plan-v04 -- the Launchpad ignores incoming clock in Programmer Mode \, so the one port fed above is pointless while it is there \, and which devices Cut It intends to DRIVE has to be decided before adding more. The Volca honors Start/Stop only when its own MIDI Clock src is set to Auto -- that is Korg's documentation and has not been measured here \, so it is a bench step rather than an assumption. See ref/device/volca.md., f 44;
