#X obj 2600 700 declare -lib pdlua;
#X text 2300 1550 Tap tempo and the phone's tempo come in here and leave on the tempo bus \, through the Lua: u_tempo.lua averages the last four intervals between taps \, starts over after two seconds \, and keeps both within the legal range \, the clip above. The timer is Pd's: t b b reads the interval \, then restarts it. A tempo the phone sends that is no number is fail u_tempo bad-tempo. ref/module/tempo.md, f 60;
#X text 2300 1850 At load the Lua answers init with a bang \, which shuts the spigot. No bang means it never loaded and fail u_tempo no-lua says so: the clock still runs \, and only the taps and the phone's tempo are dead. outlet 1 is every BPM the bus carries \, clamped \, for the phone's arrangement screen \, which u_net coalesces., f 60;
#X obj 2300 1090 t f f;
#X obj 2900 1130 outlet;
#X text 2900 1180 outlet 3 \, by its x: the tempo a tap or the phone just set \, so u_map holds knob 1 there until the knob is turned through it rather than jumping back to wherever it sits (ref/module/tempo.md). t f f: the hold \, then the bus \; nothing on the bus reaches u_map \, so either order would do. C-3, f 40;
